<!--BlockML Documentation — a guide to typed value members

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs.Properties-->
<docs:Properties xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:docs="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs"
  xmlns:dpage="org.blockml.bml.docgen.page"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation"
  xmlns:def="org.blockml.bml.definition">

  <baseType>
    dpage:DocPage
  </baseType>
  <is>
    A guide to typed value members
  </is>
  <documentation>
    Scope: typed value members, their definition, configuration, and inheritance and override,
    grounded in org.blockml.bml.definition.PropertyDefinition and the Property authoring rules in
    org.blockml.bml.documentation.MemberAuthoring. Body content is authored in a03 from those
    sources.
  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="def:PropertyDefinition" relation="Requires">
      The typed value member this page introduces.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:MemberAuthoring" relation="Requires">
      Primary content source — definition-field value versus instance-side payload syntax.
    </see>
  </references>
  <properties>
    <title override="true">
      <value>Properties</value>
    </title>
    <detailLevel override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.level.Introductory</value>
    </detailLevel>
    <pageType override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.pagetype.Concept</value>
    </pageType>
  </properties>
  <aggregations>
    <content override="true">
      <composition>
        <dpage:Section heading="What a Property is">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A Property is a Block member that carries a single scalar or structured
value. It is the piece of data a Block holds directly, as distinct from the children it owns
or the behaviour it exposes.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Declaring a typed Property with a default value">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>Declaring a Property means naming the kind of value it holds. A declaration
may also supply a default value, so that an instance which sets nothing of its own still has
a value to fall back on.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
            <dpage:CodeBlock>
              <code><![CDATA[<properties>
  <width>
    <type>type:Integer</type>
    <value>50</value>
  </width>
</properties>]]></code>
            </dpage:CodeBlock>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>In the example, the declared type names the kind of value the Property holds,
and the default value is what an instance receives when it sets nothing itself.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Definition value versus instance payload">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>The default value on a Property declaration and the value an instance
actually carries are two different things. A declaration's default lives in its own value
field, while an instance supplies the Property directly — either as an attribute on the Block
or as the property element's own content, never wrapped in a separate value element of its
own.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Every Property is optional">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A Property always behaves as optional: it either holds a value or it holds
none at all, with no separate null state to consider.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Inherited Properties and refining a default">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A Property inherited from a base Block can be refined in a child Block by
redeclaring it and supplying a new default value.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Continue with Aggregations">
          <content>
            <dpage:Navigation>
              <title>Next</title>
              <items>
                <dpage:NavigationItem label="Aggregations" summary="Learn Aggregation as an owned collection filled by composition.">
                  <target>docs:Aggregations</target>
                </dpage:NavigationItem>
              </items>
            </dpage:Navigation>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
      </composition>
    </content>
  </aggregations>
</docs:Properties>
